# What must not travel into the image.
#
# `node_modules` and `dist` are rebuilt inside the image from the lockfile; the release output, the
# test scratch dir and the review data have no business in a published artefact either.

.git
.github
node_modules
dist
extension/dist
release-assets
.tmp
reviews
spec
*.log
*.tgz
# the test-grant build (B.7.5) is a host-granted copy of extension/dist; it must never
# travel into an image, and no Docker target needs it.
extension/dist-test

# Secrets and agent-local state (SEC-26).
#
# The image is a published artefact: whatever is in the context can end up in a layer, and a layer
# cannot be un-published. Nothing below is read by `npm ci` or `npm run build` — the build needs the
# package metadata, `src/`, `scripts/` and `extension/manifest.json` — so excluding it costs nothing
# and keeps a workstation's `.env`, MCP credentials and agent settings out of the container.
#
# `**/` is spelled out because a dockerignore pattern is matched against the whole relative path:
# `.env` alone would only reach the root, and `examples/federation/.env.example` would then leak a
# sibling rule's intent. The negation re-includes the documented template and must come last.
.env
**/.env
.env.*
**/.env.*
!**/.env.example
.meta-config/secrets.local.yaml
.meta-config/env.sh
.meta-config/env.ps1
.meta-config/env.unset.sh
.meta-config/env.unset.ps1
.mcp.json
*.bluepencil.json
.claude/
.opencode/
.agent-meta/
AGENTS.personal.md
CLAUDE.local.md
CLAUDE.personal.md
*.sync-backup-*
